#Problem APEX 4.1.12

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Newton's method)
## KEYWORDS("Newton's method", 'rootfinding')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('4.1')
## Problem1('12')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric')->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.0001,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);

$a     = random(2, 5);
$f1_gr = "$a x^2";
$f2_gr = "cos(x)";

$f  = Formula("$a*x^2 - cos(x)");
$df = $f->D('x');

$a2 = 2 * $a;

@xa = (-0.5);
for my $k (0 .. 4) {
    push(@xa, $xa[$k] - $f->eval(x => $xa[$k]) / $df->eval(x => $xa[$k]));
}

@xb = (0.5);
for my $k (0 .. 4) {
    push(@xb, $xb[$k] - $f->eval(x => $xb[$k]) / $df->eval(x => $xb[$k]));
}

$ans = List($xa[-1], $xb[-1]);

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -3,
    xmax            =>  3,
    xtick_delta     =>  1,
    xminor          =>  0,
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_function(
    [ "$a*x^2", 'x', -3, 3, color => 'blue' ],
    [ "cos(x)", 'x', -3, 3, color => 'green' ]
);

$altText =
    "A graph of $a x-squared, a parabola opening up with vertex at the "
    . "origin, and the graph of cosine of x, which starts with a minimum at (-pi,-1), "
    . "passes through an intercept at x=-pi over 2, reaches a maximum at x=0, "
    . "falls through an intercept at x = pi over 2, reaching a point at (pi, -1).";

BEGIN_PGML
Use Newton's Method to approximate all
when the given functions are equal.  In other
words, solve [`f(x) = g(x)`].

Provide accuracy to 4 places after the decimal.  Use
the graph below to obtain good initial approximations.

[```
   f(x) = [$a] x^2, \quad g(x) = \cos x
```]

>> [![$altText]!]{$plot}{300} <<

[`f(x) = g(x)`] when [`x = `] [_]{$ans}{30}

  (Separate multiple answers by commas.)
END_PGML

###################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Solving [`f(x) = g(x)`] is equivalent to finding the roots
of [`h(x) = f(x) - g(x)`].

Set up the iterative formula for Newton's Method:
[```
  x_{n+1} = x_n - \dfrac{h(x_n)}{h'(x_n)} 
  = x_n - \dfrac{[$a] x_n^2 - \cos x_n}{[$a2] x_n + \sin x_n}
```]

There are two roots.  The smaller one is near [`x = [$xa[0]]`].

Using Newton's Method,
[```
  \begin{aligned}
    x_0 &= [$xa[0]] \\
    x_1 &\approx [$xa[1]] \\
    x_2 &\approx [$xa[2]] \\
    x_3 &\approx [$xa[3]]
  \end{aligned}
```]

The larger root is near [`x = [$xb[0]]`].
Using Newton's Method,
[```
  \begin{aligned}
    x_0 &= [$xb[0]] \\
    x_1 &\approx [$xb[1]] \\
    x_2 &\approx [$xb[2]] \\
    x_3 &\approx [$xb[3]]
  \end{aligned}
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
